r.chula.ac.th", "url": "https://cuir.car.chula.ac.th/xmlui/handle/123456789/20729"}, {"title": "www.silpa-mag.com", "url": "https://www.silpa-mag.com/tag/%e0%b8%88%e0%b8%b4%e0%b8%95%e0%b8%a3%e0%b8%81%e0%b8%a3%e0%b8%a3%e0%b8%a1%e0%b9%84%e0%b8%97%e0%b8%a2"}, {"title": "www.trueplookpanya.com", "url": "https://www.trueplookpanya.com/knowledge/content/58379"}]}, "The search didn't find a source that says when ภาพกาก (kak paintings) first appeared in Thai murals, so I can't give a period from these results.\n\nWhat the results do cover:\n\n- **What they are:** The term refers to figures painted in Thai murals, and a Silpa Magazine article describes งานวาดที่แฝงไว้ในจิตรกรรมไทย, which is paintings hidden within Thai murals.\n- **Appearance:** A Decorative Arts journal article says the kak figures have plain, unemotional faces, unlike the other figures, and viewers read their feelings from their posture (ตัวภาพทั้งหมดที่กล่าวมายกเว้นตัวกากน้ัน จะมหีนา้ตาทีเ่หมอืนกนัหมด, roughly: all figures except the kak share the same face).\n- **Context on dating:** Chula research on Rama IV-era murals describes three painting styles of that period, including a Western-influenced style, but these results don't link kak figures to any specific reign.\n\nFor a dated answer, I'd check the Silpa Magazine article on ภาพกาก (silpa-mag.com/?p=158656) or the Decorative Arts journal article (so07.tci-thaijo.org), which may discuss the history."], "durationSeconds": 8.473989125, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_01GceBgZEiqrQw7a31z8rLkb", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
